#message 1 "PRECINCT FRONT DESK\n\nSergeant Prakash waits behind the desk. A case folder lies beside the blinking receiver. The assignment board hangs to the left. Records is west; Market Street is east."
#message 2 "Prakash slides over case MH-041.\n\n\"Bell's Pharmacy, Market Street. Alarm at 21:20. Driver Ellis Vann is still outside. Hear his account, then check it. Photograph the lock before collecting anything.\"\n\nHe issues a camera and evidence kit. \"The board has the details. Records has the delivery ledger and dispatch log.\""
#message 3 "\"Start with Vann outside Bell's Pharmacy,\" Prakash says. \"Then speak to Bell and document the break-in. Compare their accounts with the ledger and dispatch log in Records.\""
#message 4 "MH-041: BELL'S PHARMACY\nAlarm: 21:20, rear entrance.\nDriver: Ellis Vann, waiting on Market Street.\n\nHear the driver's account. Photograph the lock; collect and label evidence. Ask the pharmacist about missing stock. Compare the delivery ledger and dispatch log in Records."
#message 5 "Prakash has a fresh report and a sharpened pencil ready. A second tray is labelled FORMS COMPLETED IN THE WRONG TRAY."
#message 6 "The brass badge reads MILLHAVEN POLICE. Officer Dana Reyes. Your first week on patrol."
#message 7 "Prakash glances at the badge. \"Reyes. Good. Let's get you an assignment.\""
#message 8 "Dispatch answers: \"Bell's Pharmacy alarm, 21:20. Driver Ellis Vann is waiting outside. The van's movements are in our log at Records. I can repeat that whenever you need it.\""
#message 9 "The portable radio is charged and tuned to Dispatch."
#message 10 "The blue desk has a receiver, a case folder and two paperwork trays. Prakash keeps the assignment board up to date."
#message 11 "Prakash holds the folder. \"Talk to me first, Reyes. You need the circumstances, not just the cover sheet.\""
#message 12 "The case report is in your kit. The desk is clear where the folder lay."
#message 13 "Case MH-041 is open. The report has spaces for the driver's account, scene photographs, labelled evidence, record checks, witness statement and arrest details."
#message 14 "\"No rush to file an empty report,\" Prakash says. \"Start with the driver and the pharmacy. Bring back the evidence; we can correct the paperwork here.\""
#message 15 "The issued camera has fresh film. Save the first photograph for the pharmacy's forced lock."
#message 16 "The evidence kit contains gloves, bags and labels. Keep the bottle's lot number readable when you find it."
#message 17 "Prakash will issue a camera and evidence kit with your assignment."
#message 18 "Ellis Vann is waiting beside his delivery van on Market Street, east of the precinct."
#message 19 "Bell's Pharmacy is on Market Street. The alarm came from its rear entrance."
#message 20 "Records keeps the delivery ledger and dispatch log. The west passage leads there."
#message 21 "Prakash says, \"A warehouse address needs a connection to the case. Compare the records with what the witnesses tell you.\""
#message 22 "No bottle has been booked into evidence. Check the pharmacy scene."
#message 23 "Prakash says, \"Names come after evidence. First find out what happened at Bell's.\""
#message 24 "Bud is out on Market Street. Dispatch says he has already asked whether the van driver wants coffee."
#message 25 "Prakash taps the second tray. \"It saves a surprising amount of explanation.\""
#message 26 "There is no scene evidence to photograph at the front desk."
#message 27 "Prakash is ready to talk. You can read the assignment board, check your badge or call Dispatch. Records is west; Market Street is east."
#message 28 "The camera and evidence kit are already packed with your report."
#message 29 "\"Keep your badge handy,\" Prakash says. \"Bell will want to know who's asking.\""
#message 30 "The desk receiver carries Dispatch's channel. Your portable radio reaches the same operator."
